('primer','The idea','Editing the model, not its prompt.', '''
<div class="eyebrow">01 / The idea</div><h1>Editing the model,<br>not its prompt.</h1>
<p class="lede">A model’s weights are learned numbers that transform inputs into outputs. Weight-space programming asks whether another model can inspect those numbers and deliberately modify them.</p>
<h2>Three ways to change behavior</h2><div class="cards"><div class="card"><span class="tag">Prompting</span><h3>Change the instructions</h3><p>The weights stay fixed. You provide different context, examples or rules at inference time.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag">Training</span><h3>Optimize the weights</h3><p>A learning procedure updates parameters to reduce a loss over data.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag good">Direct editing</span><h3>Specify a change</h3><p>An editor proposes a targeted parameter modification. Its effects still need to be tested.</p></div></div>
<h2>Why the idea is exciting</h2><p>If a small, interpretable change could reliably fix a behavior, it might be easier to audit than a broad retraining run. The difficult word is <em>reliably</em>: model internals are distributed, inputs matter, and changing one component can have unrelated consequences.</p>
<h2>What this study actually did</h2><ol class="steps"><li><b>Replay an existing edit.</b> Use the public p50c patch from the original project, rather than claim to have discovered a new one.</li><li><b>Audit its geometry.</b> Independently reconstruct the changed tensors and measure the directional transformation.</li><li><b>Compare behavior.</b> Run baseline, exact patch and a shuffled-direction control on the same four synthetic development questions.</li><li><b>Make a blind-to-outcome prediction.</b> Derive an attention-head ranking from weights, freeze it, then measure attention on twenty synthetic sequences.</li></ol>
<div class="callout warning"><strong>“Reading weights” is not introspection into this assistant.</strong><p>These experiments inspect a separate, available model checkpoint using code and analysis. They do not access the hidden weights of the frontier assistant doing the analysis.</p></div>
<h2>The missing bridge</h2><p>A weight matrix has measurable structure. Calling a direction a “repetition direction” adds a semantic claim. To connect those levels, we need controlled interventions, competing explanations and unseen inputs, not just an appealing visualization or a favorable example.</p>
<p>Here, the mathematical audit is stronger than the behavioral evidence. The separate reading experiment gives a useful failure: a plausible-looking proxy did not predict what the heads actually did.</p>
'''),
('geometry','The patch','Reversal, not erasure.', '''
<div class="eyebrow">02 / The patch</div><h1>Reversal,<br>not erasure.</h1><p class="lede">The published p50c edit touches selected columns in two tensors. Its main directional component is approximately rank one, but its strength matters.</p>
<div class="formula" aria-label="W prime equals W minus alpha u times u transpose W on selected columns">W′ = W − α u(uᵀW)<small>Applied only to the selected columns; α = 6 in the published patch.</small></div>
<p>Along the selected direction, the new component is approximately <strong>(1 − α)</strong> times the original. At α = 1 it disappears. At α = 6 it becomes −5 times its original value: reversed and amplified.</p>
<section class="interactive" aria-labelledby="slider-title"><span class="tag">Illustration, not model inference</span><h2 id="slider-title">Turn the edit strength</h2><label for="alpha">Alpha: <output id="alpha-value" for="alpha">6</output></label><input id="alpha" type="range" min="0" max="8" step="0.5" value="6"><div class="axis" aria-hidden="true"><span class="axis-zero"></span><span class="direction" id="direction"></span></div><p id="alpha-description" aria-live="polite">The selected component becomes −5× its original value: reversed and amplified.</p><p class="small">This is one-dimensional arithmetic, not a simulation of model outputs. Other components are approximately preserved; rounding and input-dependent activations still matter.</p></section>
<h2>What the independent audits established</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Measured on the realized BF16 deltas</caption><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Layer 15</th><th>Layer 19</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whole-matrix relative change</td><td>10.64%</td><td>8.11%</td></tr><tr><td>Selected-column relative change</td><td>84.98%</td><td>62.24%</td></tr><tr><td>Delta energy in leading singular component</td><td>99.999454%</td><td>99.999138%</td></tr><tr><td>Fitted α</td><td>6.000321</td><td>6.000173</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Quantized rounding gives the realized deltas a formal numerical rank greater than one. That does not mean there are many substantial mechanisms: one component accounts for nearly all their energy.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>What this does not establish</strong><p>The audit does not prove that the selected direction means repetition, or that an observed benefit is specific to repetition. Those are causal and semantic questions, not consequences of a singular-value decomposition.</p></div>
<h2>Why shuffle the direction?</h2><p>The behavioral control perturbs the same columns with a similarly sized but shuffled delta. It asks whether the original direction matters more than a nearby generic perturbation. A harmful shuffled edit is not decisive evidence for the original mechanism: it could simply damage something else.</p><p>A reverse-sign intervention was proposed in the geometry analysis, but was <strong>not run</strong>. It is not a fourth arm in the results.</p>
'''),
